      "dcterms:issued": "D:20241126",
      "dcterms:identifier": "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9810105",
      "dcterms:abstract": "The authors consider the length, lN, of the length of the longest increasing subsequence of a random permutation of N numbers. The main result in this paper is a proof that the distribution function for lN, suitably centered and scaled, converges to the Tracy-Widom distribution [TW1] of the largest eigenvalue of a random GUE matrix. The authors also prove con- vergence of moments. The proof is based on the steepest decent method for Riemann-Hilbert problems, introduced by Deift and Zhou in 1993 [DZ1] in the context of integrable systems. The applicability of the Riemann-Hilbert technique depends, in turn, on the determinantal formula of Gessel [Ge] for the Poissonization of the distribution function of lN.",
      "dcterms:source": "library/arxiv-math-9810105v2/manifest.jsonld",
      "dcterms:hasVersion": "library/arxiv-math-9810105v2"
    }
  ]
